Focal dystonia","hpo_id":"HP:0004373","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"neck onset dystonia","hpo_label":"Torticollis","hpo_id":"HP:0000473","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"GNAL","as_reported":"c.304-312delCCTCCAGTT p.P102-V104del","cdna":"c.304-312delCCTCCAGTT","protein":"p.P102-V104del","genomic":"","zygosity":"heterozygous","origin":"unknown","consequence":"in-frame deletion","key":"V8"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AD","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["c.304-312delCCTCCAGTT | p.P102-V104del","1^ | M | 20 | 56 | F | Neck |"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AD","genotype_reason":"single heterozygous variant; mode as stated for the family"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:23222958:1","pmid":"23222958","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Fuchs et al. (2013) used exome sequencing in 2 families with primary torsion dystonia and identified heterozygous GNAL mutations: a nonsense mutation (S293X) in one family and a missense mutation (V137M) in the other. Sanger sequencing of 39 additional multiplex PTD families negative for DYT1 and DYT6 found GNAL mutations in 6 more families (19%). None of the variants were present in dbSNP135, about 3,500 European exomes or 572 control chromosomes.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["Using exome sequencing in two families with PTD, we identified a new causative gene, GNAL, with a nonsense mutation encoding p.Ser293* resulting in a premature stop codon in one family and a missense mutation encoding p.Val137Met in the other.","We detected novel mutations in the GNAL gene in six additional families (19%)","None of the variants were detected in dbSNP135, ~3,500 European exomes or 572 control chromosomes that we tested."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23222958:2","pmid":"23222958","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Fuchs et al. (2013) reported 28 mutation carriers with definite dystonia from 8 families. Mean age at onset was 31.3 years (range 7 to 54). Onset was in the neck in 82%, and 93% had neck involvement at last exam, but only 46% still had focal dystonia. Cranial involvement was seen in 57% and speech involvement in 44%; no patient had brachial onset, and only 32% developed arm involvement, which distinguished GNAL dystonia from DYT6.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["Among the 8 families with mutations there were 28 individuals with complete clinical information who had definite dystonia","Average age of dystonia onset in the carriers was 31.3, and ranged from 7–54 years. Most carriers (82%) had onset in the neck, and 93% had neck involvement at final exam, however most progressed to have dystonia at other sites, and only 46% had focal dystonia at last exam.","Brachial onset was not observed and eventual arm involvement was seen in only 32%, distinguishing GNAL from DYT6"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23222958:3","pmid":"23222958","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Fuchs et al. (2013) found no genotype/phenotype correlation by mutation type, and phenotypes varied within families carrying the same mutation. Dystonia was inherited equally from the maternal and paternal sides, with no apparent phenotypic difference by parental origin, although the authors said further study was needed to determine whether parental origin affects penetrance or expression.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["Dystonia was inherited from maternal and paternal sides equally, and there were no apparent phenotypic differences between maternally and paternally inherited cases.","no genotype/phenotype correlation could be discerned with regard to mutation type and, as seen in Table 1 and Supplementary Table 1, phenotypes varied within families with the same mutation."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23222958:4","pmid":"23222958","section":"gene_function","statement":"Fuchs et al. (2013) studied the mutants with a BRET assay of Galpha-olf coupling to the D1 dopamine receptor. The S293X mutant failed to support any D1R-driven responses. The patient mutants E155K and V137M showed intermediate phenotypes largely consistent with impaired association with G-beta-gamma subunits, while the control variant V16F behaved like wildtype. The authors suggested the results pointed to loss of function.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["The p.S293X deletion mutant failed to support any D1R driven responses while p.V16F, a variant found in a single control sample showed normal wild-type like behavior.","Two mutants found in dystonia patients, p.E155K and p.V137M, showed intermediate phenotypes largely consistent with impaired association with the Gβγ subunits (Fig. 2).","A functional assay testing several of the mutations is suggestive of a loss of function."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23222958:5","pmid":"23222958","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Fuchs et al. (2013) noted that Galpha-olf replaces Galpha-s in striatal medium spiny neurons, where it couples D1 dopamine and A2A adenosine receptors to adenylyl cyclase type 5. They suggested that GNAL mutations point to primary abnormalities of D1R and/or A2AR transmission, located postsynaptically in striatal dopaminoceptive neurons and/or cholinergic interneurons.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["In MSNs, Gαolf couples dopamine type 1 receptors (D1R) of the direct pathway and adenosine A2A receptors (A2AR) of the indirect pathway to the activation of adenylyl cyclase type 5 (AC5)–.","Identification of causative mutations in GNAL points to primary abnormalities of D1R and/or A2AR transmission as possibly leading to dystonia.","GNAL mutations place the primary abnormality post-synaptically in striatal dopaminoceptive neurons and/or cholinergic interneurons."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23222958:6","pmid":"23222958","section":"mapping","statement":"Fuchs et al. (2013) noted that GNAL lies on chromosome 18p, centromeric to the DYT7 and DYT15 loci. They suggested that loss of GNAL may contribute to the dystonia seen in patients with 18p deletions.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["GNAL is located on chromosome 18p centromeric to the DYT7 locus for focal dystonia and the DYT15 locus for myoclonus dystonia,. Dystonia occurs in patients with 18p deletions–, and absence of GNAL may be contributing to dystonia in these cases."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:8309575:1","pmid":"8309575","section":"heterogeneity","statement":"Bressman et al. (1994) used linkage data to exclude the DYT1 region on chromosome 9q34 in a non-Jewish North American family with adult-onset torsion dystonia that mainly began in the neck. They concluded that a gene other than DYT1 causes some cases of adult cervical-onset dystonia.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Linkage data excluded the region containing the DYT1 locus, indicating that DYT1 was not responsible for ITD in this family.","This study provides evidence that a gene other than DYT1 is responsible for some cases of adult cervical-onset dystonia."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["GNAL"],"confirmed":["GNAL"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AD"]}